1. `std::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(data, ctx.buffer.as_mut_ptr().add(..), chunk)` copies bytes from a raw pointer into the buffer. With `data: &[u8]`, this becomes `copy_from_slice` on two subslices.
2. `data = data.add(chunk)` advances the pointer. With a slice, this becomes `data = &data[chunk..]`.
3. `data` is `Arr`, `Imm`, `Transient`, so it maps to `&[u8]`, and the separate `len` argument is folded into `data.len()`.
4. `ctx` is `Ptr`, `Mut`, `Transient`, so it stays `&mut md5_ctx`.
5. `let block = ctx.buffer` copies the array (`[u8; 64]` is `Copy`). That copy is needed so `ctx` can be borrowed mutably by `md5_transform`, so I keep it.

No `unsafe` remains. The `bit_len` update uses `data.len()` before the loop, as in the original.

----FUNCTION----
```rust
pub fn md5_update(ctx: &mut md5_ctx, data: &[u8]) {
    let mut data = data;
    ctx.bit_len = ctx.bit_len.wrapping_add((data.len() as u64).wrapping_mul(8u64));
    while !data.is_empty() {
        let space: usize = ctx.buffer.len() - ctx.buffer_len;
        let chunk: usize = data.len().min(space);
        let start = ctx.buffer_len;
        ctx.buffer[start..start + chunk].copy_from_slice(&data[..chunk]);
        ctx.buffer_len += chunk;
        data = &data[chunk..];
        if ctx.buffer_len == ctx.buffer.len() {
            let block: [u8; 64] = ctx.buffer;
            md5_transform(ctx, &block);
            ctx.buffer_len = 0;
        }
    }
}
